<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mersa Gawasis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wadi Gawasis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mersa Gawasis is an archaeological site on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, south of Safaga, where a harbour was used by Middle Kingdom expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> and other lands. Excavations since 2001 have found man-made caves with ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a>, coiled ropes, cargo boxes and inscriptions naming Punt. The ships were built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley, carried across the desert in pieces and reassembled on the coast.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The harbour, known in ancient Egyptian as Saww, lies at the mouth of Wadi Gawasis, 2 km south of the mouth of Wadi Gasus, with Safaga 25 km to the north and al-Qusair 50 km to the south. Its name joins mersa, port, to gawasis, a medieval term for a scout boat. It appears to have been used as a port from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a> of the 12th Dynasty, as a loading point for voyages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a> and as a starting point for journeys to the mines of Sinai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The search began when a 12th Dynasty stele naming a nearby port called Saww was found at a Graeco-Roman watering station in Wadi Gasus. The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, under Abdel Monem A.H. Sayed, dug there and then at the mouth of Mersa Gasus without finding pharaonic remains, and concluded that the stele had been carried from Saww in Roman times. Near Mersa Gawasis a small stele bore the cartouche of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> and the place name Bia-n-punt, and a shrine of Ankhu, valet of Senusret I, built on a limestone anchor, again named Sww.</p><p>A further stele on an anchor base recorded an order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-i">Senusret I</a> to the vizier Intefiqer for a ship to sail to Bia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>. In 1977 potsherds with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hieratic">hieratic</a> inscriptions listing the contents, origin and destination of their cargoes were found, naming a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-ii">Senusret II</a>, Punt and an official of the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a>. These finds identified Saww with Mersa Gawasis and showed that Middle Kingdom ships sailed from here to Punt and back, and that on their return they were taken apart and carried to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-valley">Nile Valley</a> in pieces. The anchors, of about 110 kg, were made on the spot and left behind, some reused as bases for shrines and stelae.</p><p>In 2005 an expedition led by the archaeologist Kathryn Bard found the remains of seagoing vessels and stelae with hieroglyphic texts on royal expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>, some 1200 km to the south, among them cedar planks from Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatshepsut">Hatshepsut</a>'s expedition of the 15th century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mersa Gawasis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The earliest recorded expedition went out under Sahure of the 5th Dynasty, c. 2450 BCE, and later voyages sailed from Red Sea harbours such as <b>Mersa Gawasis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/land-of-punt">Land of Punt</a></li></ul></section>
